  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "Suppose that the function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 5-2x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function?",
  "worked_solution": "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at $x=1.$ To see this, we note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2\\ne 3={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-2,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,3]$ and $[-2,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,1]$ and $[0,3]$ contain the point $x=1.$",
